---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';

const snapshot = [
  { label: 'Organization', value: 'Dental clinic network, 6 locations across Lombardy and Piedmont' },
  { label: 'Timeframe', value: 'Ongoing relationship since 2022 — detailed data 2024–2025' },
  { label: 'Role', value: 'Digital Advertising Specialist: strategy, campaigns, reporting, team training' },
  { label: 'Channels', value: 'Meta Ads (primary), Google Ads Search' },
  { label: 'Team', value: 'Project manager, videographer, front-desk staff at each location' },
];

const results = [
  { metric: '13.55×', label: '2024 program ROAS' },
  { metric: '293', label: 'leads generated in 2024' },
  { metric: '~19.9×', label: 'best single event ROAS' },
];
---
<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Turning a recurring events format into a measurable acquisition channel"
  description="Case study: an Open Day program across a multi-location network, measured from the first click to the acquired patient. Meta Ads, 13.55x ROAS."
  eyebrow="Meta Ads · End-to-end measurement"
  snapshot={snapshot}
  results={results}
>
  <h2 class="fade-in">The context</h2>
  <p>
    The client runs a dental network with 6 locations and a recurring format: themed "Open Days"
    — implantology, orthodontics, gnathology, cosmetic dentistry — held periodically across the
    different sites. Not a one-off campaign. An ongoing program, repeated several times a year,
    city after city.
  </p>

  <h2 class="fade-in">The challenge</h2>
  <p>
    Counting form submissions is easy. The real question was different: how many of those leads
    actually turn into patients — event by event, location by location? And why does the exact
    same format perform so differently depending on where and when it runs?
  </p>

  <h2 class="fade-in">My role</h2>
  <p>
    Each Open Day was shaped together with the project manager and the team, with photo and video
    produced alongside the videographer. From there, it was mine to run: building and optimizing
    Meta campaigns event by event and location by location, reporting back to the client on a
    regular cadence, and — the part I care about most — training front-desk staff at every
    location on handling incoming leads. The best campaign in the world is worthless if the
    contact isn't handled well.
  </p>

  <h2 class="fade-in">Analysis and insight</h2>
  <p>
    Cost per lead swung wildly between locations — from €6.50 to over €80 in the same period.
    Read only in aggregate, that gap would have gone unnoticed. Broken down location by location,
    it showed that timing and local saturation moved the needle as much as the creative did.
  </p>
  <p class="open-point">
    One limitation, stated plainly: where and when to run events was the brand's call, not mine.
    Geographic budget allocation wasn't a lever I could pull. Knowing what you control from what
    you don't is part of the analysis.
  </p>

  <h2 class="fade-in">Execution</h2>
  <p>
    16-plus lead-gen campaigns for individual events between 2022 and 2026, each with creative and
    budget tailored to its location. Alongside them: low-cost local awareness campaigns — over 1.9
    million people reached for roughly €1,100 total — and, over time, automation flows routing
    leads straight to the front desks, which helped push ROAS higher.
  </p>

  <h2 class="fade-in">Results</h2>
  <p>
    In 2024 the program generated 293 leads on €2,838.64 in spend and €38,473 in attributed
    revenue — a ROAS of roughly 13.55×. The single best-performing event hit around 19.9× on under
    €800 invested. By May 2025, with data still partial, the program had already produced 192
    leads, and the pipeline was still closing.
  </p>

  <h2 class="fade-in">What I learned</h2>
  <p>
    How to manage a growing spend without losing efficiency. How to build process, not just
    campaigns — lead automation and front-desk training moved the result as much as media
    optimization did. And if I were starting over, I'd nail down the content creation process
    first: spotting a winning creative fast and reproducing it would have let us scale the best
    results instead of starting from zero every single time.
  </p>
</CaseStudyLayout>
